tongxin-009:
**Key idea:** Most synchronous-FIFO bugs come from (1) full/empty that cannot be told apart, (2) pointer width/wrap errors, (3) updating state on *requests* instead of *accepted* operations, and (4) blocking assignments or multiple drivers on the same register. Fix by computing `do_wr`/`do_rd` once and deriving everything from them with nonblocking assignments.

### Typical buggy RTL (interview version) and what is wrong
```verilog
module fifo_bug #(parameter W = 8, D = 16) (
  input clk, rst_n, wr_en, rd_en, input [W-1:0] din,
  output reg [W-1:0] dout, output full, empty);
  reg [W-1:0] mem [0:D-1];
  reg [3:0] wptr, rptr;                       // (1) no wrap bit
  always @(posedge clk) begin                 // (2) no reset
    if (wr_en) begin                          // (3) writes even when full
      mem[wptr] = din;                        // (4) blocking assignment
      wptr = wptr + 1;
    end
    if (rd_en) begin                          // (3) reads even when empty
      dout = mem[rptr];
      rptr = rptr + 1;
    end
  end
  assign full  = (wptr == rptr);              // (5) identical to empty
  assign empty = (wptr == rptr);
endmodule
```

| # | Bug | Symptom | Fix |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Pointers only `log2(D)` bits | full and empty indistinguishable | Extra MSB (wrap bit) or occupancy counter |
| 2 | No reset | X pointers at start | Async/sync reset of pointers |
| 3 | Uses `wr_en`/`rd_en` directly | overflow overwrites oldest data; underflow corrupts `rptr` | `do_wr = wr_en & !full`, `do_rd = rd_en & !empty` |
| 4 | Blocking `=` in clocked block | races with other processes reading `wptr`/`dout` | `<=` for all sequential state |
| 5 | Same compare for full/empty | FIFO looks full when empty | MSBs differ & rest equal → full; all equal → empty |

### Code (fixed, power-of-two depth, 1-cycle registered read)
```systemverilog
module sync_fifo_fixed #(parameter int W = 8, parameter int D = 16) (  // D must be 2**n
  input  logic         clk, rst_n, wr_en, rd_en,
  input  logic [W-1:0] din,
  output logic [W-1:0] dout,            // valid the cycle after an accepted read
  output logic         full, empty
);
  localparam int AW = $clog2(D);
  logic [W-1:0] mem [D];
  logic [AW:0]  wptr, rptr;             // extra wrap bit

  assign empty = (wptr == rptr);
  assign full  = (wptr[AW] != rptr[AW]) && (wptr[AW-1:0] == rptr[AW-1:0]);

  wire do_wr = wr_en && !full;
  wire do_rd = rd_en && !empty;

  always_ff @(posedge clk)
    if (do_wr) mem[wptr[AW-1:0]] <= din;

  always_ff @(posedge clk or negedge rst_n)
    if (!rst_n) begin
      wptr <= '0;
      rptr <= '0;
      dout <= '0;
    end else begin
      if (do_wr) wptr <= wptr + 1'b1;
      if (do_rd) begin
        dout <= mem[rptr[AW-1:0]];
        rptr <= rptr + 1'b1;
      end
    end

  // embedded checks
  a_no_ovf: assert property (@(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n) full  && wr_en && !rd_en |=> $stable(wptr));
  a_no_udf: assert property (@(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n) empty && rd_en |=> $stable(rptr));
  a_excl:   assert property (@(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n) !(full && empty));
endmodule
```

### How to debug in an interview
- Draw pointer values for D=4 through fill-to-full and drain-to-empty; the bug in (5) shows immediately.
- Check read latency vs. the interface spec (here 1 cycle; FWFT needs `assign dout = mem[rptr]`).
- Same-address read+write cannot happen here: read is blocked when empty, write is blocked when full.

### Follow-ups
- *Non-power-of-two depth?* Use an occupancy counter and explicit wrap at `D-1`.
- *Count output?* `wptr - rptr` (AW+1 bits) is the occupancy for free.

tongxin-010:
**Key idea:** A power manager is a sequencer in an **always-on** domain. Bugs usually are (1) asynchronous request/power-good inputs used without synchronizers, (2) blocking assignments in the clocked state register (or nonblocking in combinational next-state logic), and (3) wrong ordering of clock-stop, isolation, retention, and power-switch control. Fix all three and drive every control output from a register so it cannot glitch.

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
  [*] --> RUN
  RUN --> STOP_CLK: sleep_req_s
  STOP_CLK --> ISO
  ISO --> SAVE
  SAVE --> OFF
  OFF --> PWR_UP: wake_req_s
  PWR_UP --> RESTORE: pwr_good_s
  RESTORE --> UNISO
  UNISO --> RUN
```

### Typical bugs and fixes

| Bug | Why it fails | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| `if (sleep_req)` on a signal from another clock | metastability, different flops see different values | 2-FF synchronizer in the always-on clock |
| `state = nxt;` (blocking) in `always @(posedge clk)` | other blocks read the new value in the same step → race | `state <= nxt;` |
| `nxt <= ...` in `always @*` / missing default | latch inferred, sim/synth mismatch | blocking with defaults in `always_comb` |
| Outputs decoded combinationally from binary state | glitch on `iso_en` / `pwr_on` during state change | register outputs from `nxt` |
| Power off before isolation | off-domain outputs float/X into on-domain | ISO before switch-off, de-ISO after restore |
| Proceed on `pwr_on` without waiting for power-good | logic starts on a ramping rail | wait for synchronized `pwr_good` |
| Short wake pulse from fast domain | lost by level synchronizer | stretch / toggle-handshake, or a sticky request cleared by ack |

### Code (fixed)
```systemverilog
module pwr_mgr (
  input  logic clk, rst_n,               // always-on clock; rst_n already sync-deasserted
  input  logic sleep_req_a, wake_req_a,  // levels from other domains (held until ack)
  input  logic pwr_good_a,               // from power switch
  output logic clk_en, iso_en, save, restore, pwr_on, dom_rst_n
);
  typedef enum logic [2:0] {RUN, STOP_CLK, ISO, SAVE, OFF, PWR_UP, RESTORE, UNISO} st_t;
  st_t st, nxt;

  // 2-FF synchronizers (one per independent level signal)
  logic [1:0] s_sleep, s_wake, s_pg;
  always_ff @(posedge clk or negedge rst_n)
    if (!rst_n) {s_sleep, s_wake, s_pg} <= '0;
    else begin
      s_sleep <= {s_sleep[0], sleep_req_a};
      s_wake  <= {s_wake[0],  wake_req_a};
      s_pg    <= {s_pg[0],    pwr_good_a};
    end
  wire sleep_s = s_sleep[1], wake_s = s_wake[1], pg_s = s_pg[1];

  // next-state: blocking, complete
  always_comb begin
    nxt = st;
    unique case (st)
      RUN:      if (sleep_s) nxt = STOP_CLK;
      STOP_CLK: nxt = ISO;
      ISO:      nxt = SAVE;
      SAVE:     nxt = OFF;
      OFF:      if (wake_s)  nxt = PWR_UP;
      PWR_UP:   if (pg_s)    nxt = RESTORE;
      RESTORE:  nxt = UNISO;
      UNISO:    nxt = RUN;
      default:  nxt = RUN;
    endcase
  end

  // state + registered (glitch-free) outputs: nonblocking
  always_ff @(posedge clk or negedge rst_n)
    if (!rst_n) begin
      st <= RUN;  clk_en <= 1'b1; iso_en <= 1'b0; save <= 1'b0;
      restore <= 1'b0; pwr_on <= 1'b1; dom_rst_n <= 1'b0;
    end else begin
      st        <= nxt;
      clk_en    <= (nxt == RUN);
      iso_en    <= (nxt inside {ISO, SAVE, OFF, PWR_UP, RESTORE});
      save      <= (nxt == SAVE);
      restore   <= (nxt == RESTORE);
      pwr_on    <= !(nxt == OFF);
      dom_rst_n <= !(nxt inside {OFF, PWR_UP});   // hold domain in reset while unpowered
    end
endmodule
```
Multi-cycle waits (regulator settle, save duration) are added with a counter per state when the spec requires them.

### Verify
- Assertions: `!pwr_on |-> iso_en`; `$fell(pwr_on) |-> $past(iso_en)`; `$rose(clk_en) |-> pg_s`.
- Power-aware simulation with UPF: corruption of the off domain, isolation clamps, retention restore values.
- CDC lint on all asynchronous inputs; random request timing including requests during transitions.

### Follow-ups
- *Sleep request arrives during power-up?* Spec decides: finish wake then honor, or abort — must be explicit and covered.

tongxin-024:
**Key idea:** Caches differ by **placement** (direct-mapped, N-way set-associative, fully associative), **organization** (split I/D vs unified, levels), and **policies**: write hit (write-back vs write-through) and write miss (write-allocate vs no-write-allocate). Write-back writes only the cache and sets a **dirty** bit; the line goes to memory when it is evicted.

### Comparison

| Type | Where a block may go | Hit time | Conflict misses | Cost |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Direct-mapped | exactly 1 line | fastest | highest | 1 tag compare |
| N-way set-assoc. | any of N ways in 1 set | medium | lower | N comparators + replacement |
| Fully assoc. | anywhere | slowest | none | CAM, used for TLBs/small buffers |

| Write policy | Memory traffic | Consistency w/ memory | Needs |
|---|---|---|---|
| Write-through | every store | always up to date | write buffer |
| Write-back | only dirty evictions | memory stale until evict | dirty bit, write-back on eviction/flush |
| Write-allocate | fetch line on write miss | — | usually paired with write-back |
| No-write-allocate | write goes around cache | — | usually paired with write-through |

### Code: write-back, write-allocate reference model (for a scoreboard)
```systemverilog
class wb_cache_model #(int LINES = 64, int LINE_BYTES = 16);
  localparam int OFF_W = $clog2(LINE_BYTES);
  localparam int IDX_W = $clog2(LINES);

  typedef struct {
    bit             valid, dirty;
    bit [31:0]      tag;
    byte unsigned   data[LINE_BYTES];
  } line_t;

  line_t        lines[LINES];
  byte unsigned mem[bit [31:0]];          // sparse backing memory (unwritten = 0)
  int           hits, misses, writebacks;

  local function void split(bit [31:0] a, output bit [31:0] tag,
                            output int idx, output int off);
    off = a[OFF_W-1:0];
    idx = (a >> OFF_W) & (LINES-1);
    tag = a >> (OFF_W + IDX_W);
  endfunction

  local function bit [31:0] line_base(bit [31:0] tag, int idx);
    return (tag << (OFF_W + IDX_W)) | (32'(idx) << OFF_W);
  endfunction

  // make line idx hold tag: write back a dirty victim, then fill
  local function void lookup(bit [31:0] tag, int idx);
    if (lines[idx].valid && lines[idx].tag == tag) begin hits++; return; end
    misses++;
    if (lines[idx].valid && lines[idx].dirty) begin
      bit [31:0] vb = line_base(lines[idx].tag, idx);
      foreach (lines[idx].data[i]) mem[vb + i] = lines[idx].data[i];
      writebacks++;
    end
    begin
      bit [31:0] nb = line_base(tag, idx);
      foreach (lines[idx].data[i])
        lines[idx].data[i] = mem.exists(nb + i) ? mem[nb + i] : 8'h00;
    end
    lines[idx].valid = 1; lines[idx].dirty = 0; lines[idx].tag = tag;
  endfunction

  function byte unsigned read(bit [31:0] addr);
    bit [31:0] tag; int idx, off;
    split(addr, tag, idx, off);
    lookup(tag, idx);
    return lines[idx].data[off];
  endfunction

  function void write(bit [31:0] addr, byte unsigned d);   // write-allocate
    bit [31:0] tag; int idx, off;
    split(addr, tag, idx, off);
    lookup(tag, idx);
    lines[idx].data[off] = d;
    lines[idx].dirty     = 1;                              // write-back: memory not updated
  endfunction
endclass
```

### Worked example (direct-mapped, 64 lines × 16 B)
`write 0x0000_0010` → miss, fill line 1, dirty. `read 0x0000_0410` → same index 1, different tag → **write back** line 1 to memory, then fill. Memory sees one 16-byte write instead of one per store.

### Follow-ups
- *Why split L1 I/D?* Parallel fetch and data access (no structural hazard), different policies; L2 usually unified.
- *Write-back pitfall:* DMA reading memory must see dirty data → clean the cache or use coherent IO.

tongxin-034:
**Key idea:** Model the lock as a sequence detector over **key events**: each state = how many leading digits of the code have been matched. A correct key advances; a wrong key falls back to the longest prefix still matched (for code `3-5-1-7`, a wrong key that equals `3` goes to "1 matched", anything else to IDLE). Add an inter-key timeout and a fixed-length unlock pulse. The code and timing policy below are chosen and stated because the question gives none.

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
  [*] --> IDLE
  IDLE --> G1: key==3
  G1 --> G2: key==5
  G2 --> G3: key==1
  G3 --> OPEN: key==7
  G1 --> IDLE: wrong key or timeout
  G2 --> IDLE: wrong key or timeout
  G3 --> IDLE: wrong key or timeout
  OPEN --> IDLE: OPEN_CYC elapsed
```
(A wrong key equal to `3` goes to G1 instead of IDLE.)

### Code
```systemverilog
module digital_lock #(
  parameter logic [3:0] C0 = 4'd3, C1 = 4'd5, C2 = 4'd1, C3 = 4'd7,
  parameter int TMO = 1000,      // max cycles between keys
  parameter int OPEN_CYC = 50    // unlock pulse length
) (
  input  logic       clk, rst_n,
  input  logic       key_vld,    // one-cycle pulse per debounced key press
  input  logic [3:0] key,
  output logic       unlock
);
  typedef enum logic [2:0] {IDLE, G1, G2, G3, OPEN} st_t;
  st_t st, nst;
  logic [$clog2(TMO+OPEN_CYC+1)-1:0] tmr;

  wire timeout = (st inside {G1, G2, G3}) && (tmr == TMO-1) && !key_vld;
  wire open_done = (st == OPEN) && (tmr == OPEN_CYC-1);

  always_comb begin
    nst = st;
    if (timeout) nst = IDLE;
    else unique case (st)
      IDLE: if (key_vld) nst = (key == C0) ? G1 : IDLE;
      G1:   if (key_vld) nst = (key == C1) ? G2 : (key == C0) ? G1 : IDLE;
      G2:   if (key_vld) nst = (key == C2) ? G3 : (key == C0) ? G1 : IDLE;
      G3:   if (key_vld) nst = (key == C3) ? OPEN : (key == C0) ? G1 : IDLE;
      OPEN: if (open_done) nst = IDLE;              // keys ignored while open
      default: nst = IDLE;
    endcase
  end

  always_ff @(posedge clk or negedge rst_n)
    if (!rst_n) begin
      st <= IDLE; tmr <= '0;
    end else begin
      st  <= nst;
      tmr <= (nst != st || (key_vld && st != OPEN)) ? '0 : tmr + 1'b1;  // restart on state change / key
    end

  assign unlock = (st == OPEN);
endmodule
```

### Assertions
```systemverilog
// unlock only right after the full code
a_only_after_code: assert property (@(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n)
  $rose(unlock) |-> $past(key_vld && key == C3 && st == G3));
// unlock lasts exactly OPEN_CYC cycles
a_len: assert property (@(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n)
  $rose(unlock) |-> unlock [*OPEN_CYC] ##1 !unlock);
// no unlock across a timeout
a_tmo: assert property (@(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n)
  timeout |=> st == IDLE);
c_retry: cover property (@(posedge clk) st == G2 ##1 st == G1 ##[1:$] unlock);
```

### Test ideas
- Exact code; code with a wrong digit at each position; `3 3 5 1 7` (repeat of first digit) must unlock; timeout at TMO−1 (still OK) and TMO (fails); reset while OPEN; keys during OPEN.

### Follow-ups
- *Code with self-overlap (e.g. 1-1-2)?* Use KMP-style failure transitions (longest suffix that is a prefix).
- *Lockout after 3 failures?* Add a failure counter and a LOCKOUT state.
